<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.CSS_Name_GridRow @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Api_Usage</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.CSS_Name_GridRow @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Api_Usage</h1>

<p>
    Everything you need to work with the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-grid-row</span> CSS class: the BEM classes it provides, the strongly-typed <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">GridRow</span> C# object
    used to apply them, supporting enums, and the CSS variables used for styling.
</p>

<h2 class="mobile-centred-heading">Overview</h2>
<p>
    <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-grid-row</span> is a 12-column CSS Grid container. Children are sized across the grid using the
    <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-grid-col-*</span> classes generated by <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">GridRow.ColSpan</span>, one per breakpoint you want to
    override at.
</p>
<p>
    Breakpoints are min-width media queries: a span applies from its breakpoint upward until a wider breakpoint overrides it. There's no need to
    specify a class at every breakpoint - only add a <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-grid-col-*</span> class for the widths where the span should change.
</p>
<p>
    Unlike the container-query-driven grid used internally by <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">BlazorRamp.Inputs</span>, <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-grid-row</span> responds to the
    viewport's width via media queries, not the width of the row itself.
</p>

<p>
    Because <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-grid-row</span> creates implicit rows automatically via <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">grid-auto-rows</span>, you can technically
    place everything in a single <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-grid-row</span> and let content wrap onto new rows on its own as columns exceed 12 in the current line.
    This has a genuine advantage: because every "row" shares the same 12 column tracks, columns line up vertically across the whole grid without any
    extra effort - something a flexbox or float-based row system can't offer, since each of its rows is an independent container with no relationship
    to the ones around it.
</p>
<p>
    The trade-off is that the browser's default placement is a <i>greedy pack</i>, not a "wrap after N items" - it only starts a new row when there
    genuinely isn't room left on the current one. That means you can't force a deliberate break at a specific point without either an item wide enough
    to force the wrap or explicit <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">grid-row</span> positioning, so reordering content, adding an item, or resizing the viewport can shift exactly
    what ends up on which row. A single shared grid also makes it harder to scope per-row concerns - a different gap on one section, a background or
    border around just one row, or wrapping a landmark/fieldset around one row's items - without introducing another grid context anyway.
</p>
<p>
    For most layouts - anything where a "row" is a meaningful group, like a form section or a distinct block of content - use one
    <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-grid-row</span> per row. It gives deterministic breaks regardless of viewport width and keeps the DOM grouping meaningful for
    accessibility purposes. Reach for a single row with many columns instead when the content is genuinely repetitive and ungrouped - a card grid, a
    photo gallery, dashboard tiles - where consistent column alignment across the whole set is the point and there's no semantic grouping to lose.
</p>

<section>
    <h2 id="c#-classes" class="mobile-centred-heading">C# API</h2>
    <p>
        <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">GridRow</span> provides the classes for the br-grid-row block: a 12-column grid
        container, and the column span classes used to size its children.
    </p>
    <section>
        <h3 id="gridrow-class" class="mobile-centred-heading">GridRow class</h3>
        <div class="@Section.Base @Section.NoPadding @Section.OverflowX(UnitScroll.Auto) @Padding.Block(UnitSpace.Four)">
            <table class="@Table.Base @Table.CodeTable" tabindex="0" aria-labelledby="gridrow-class">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th scope="col">Return</th>
                        <th scope="col">Const/Static Method</th>
                        <th scope="col">Description</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td>string</td>
                        <td>GridRow.Base</td>
                        <td>The base class, <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">br-grid-row</span>. Always required on the container element.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>string</td>
                        <td>GridRow.NoMarginBlock</td>
                        <td>Removes the row's default block-axis margin.</td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td>string</td>
                        <td>GridRow.ColSpan(UnitBreakpoint unitBreakpoint, UnitColSpan unitColSpan)</td>
                        <td>Gets the class that sets a child element's column span at and above the given breakpoint.</td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

